  "notes": "One-single piano pop — the purest \"sad-but-bouncy\" 2000s adult-contemporary record: about 70 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and a 16th piano subdivision. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and 3\", \"clap-layered snare on 2&4\" (the clap offset tightened), and \"8th hat plus tambourine from the chorus\"; the fourth fact, \"the drums drop to hat-only in the bridge\", is `dropOutBeats` at 0.9. The rhythm engine is not the kit but \"staccato block-chord piano\", which is why the harmonic rhythm is pinned to one chord a bar over a four-beat duration and the chord voicing is close rather than open. Harmony is I–V–vi–IV under every section with a suspended pre-chorus and no modulation. The melody is \"built on a repeated descending 3-note cell\", which is `motifPitchCount` 3–4 with a descend-dominant contour, and the \"'whoa-oh' gang vocal used as an instrumental hook\" is the octave-echo counter. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research says so: only the tempo is sourced and the remaining parameters are convention-derived from the record's documented lane, and the key claims are marked **[LOW-CONF]**, which is why `keys` is left inherited. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are the ±4 BPM spread around the stated ~70 and every drum probability.",
